    "created_at": "2018-11-27T01:58:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-11-27T01:58:22Z",
    "body": "I'm not sure this is right. Per the spec after this PR, if a media element is eligible for autoplay, then it is allowed to play.\r\n\r\nBut, from what I can tell, because the autoplay policy has a default allowlist of \"self\", any `<audio paused>` elements in a top-level document are eligible for autoplay. And thus would be allowed to play.\r\n\r\nI don't think is what browsers implement.\r\n\r\nMaybe it would help to outline the actual algorithm implemented for \"allowed to play\" in browsers? I.e., under what conditions does `play()` throw a \"`NotAllowedError`\" `DOMException?\r\n\r\nI cannot find anywhere in the Blink code, for example, that consults feature policy, or the paused attribute, or any of the other things that go into the definition of \"eligible for autoplay\".",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "reactions": {
